In this illustrative example, 18:00 performs best.

The system should not simply conclude that 18:00 is universally best. It should investigate whether that result is consistent across comparable posts, days and platforms.

### Scheduling intelligence

Build an algorithm that calculates an optimal publishing window using:

| Factor                   | Weight |
| ------------------------ | ------ |
| Historical views         | 20%    |
| Average watch duration   | 20%    |
| Audience retention       | 20%    |
| Engagement               | 15%    |
| Follower activity        | 15%    |
| Recent performance trend | 10%    |

These are initial configurable weights, not proven universal predictors. The system should eventually learn the relative importance of features using historical data and controlled experiments.

It should also consider:

- Day of the week.
- Local timezone.
- Time since the last post.
- Platform-specific audience behavior.
- Content category.
- Video duration.
- Publishing frequency.
- Sample size and statistical confidence.

### Three scheduling modes

Mode 1

Manual scheduling

You choose all dates and times.

Mode 2

AI recommendations

The system suggests the best time and explains why. You approve the changes.

Mode 3

Autopilot scheduling

The system automatically chooses and adjusts future publishing slots within your configured limits.

One important rule: Autopilot must never silently reschedule a post that's already being uploaded or published. It should only modify eligible future scheduled posts, respecting your minimum spacing, maximum posts per day and blackout periods.

### Continuous learning

The system should maintain a performance history for every recommended time slot.

For example:

- Monday at 18:00: consistently strong.
- Tuesday at 18:00: average.
- Friday at 21:00: strong.
- Saturday at 12:00: weak.

It should also experiment with alternative slots instead of endlessly repeating the same time. This avoids getting stuck with an initially lucky result.

Use controlled comparisons, preferably comparing similar content, with confidence estimates and minimum sample requirements.

